<DocsLayout title="Organizations & Members">
  <h1>Organizations & Members</h1>
  <p class="text-lg text-slate-400">Workspaces, team collaboration, and role-based access control.</p>

  <h2>Organizations</h2>
  <p>An <strong>organization</strong> is the top-level workspace in Datanika. All resources — connections, uploads, transformations, pipelines, and schedules — belong to an organization. Data is fully isolated between organizations.</p>

  <h3>Properties</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>Display name of the organization (e.g., "Acme Corp")</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>slug</code></td><td>Unique URL-safe identifier (e.g., "acme-corp")</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>default_dbt_schema</code></td><td>Default schema name for dbt projects (default: "datanika")</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Example</h3>
  <p>When you sign up, you create your first organization. You can then invite team members and assign roles.</p>
  <pre><code set:html={`Organization: "My Data Team"
Slug: my-data-team
Default dbt schema: analytics`} /></pre>

  <h2>Members & Invitations</h2>
  <p>Each user can belong to multiple organizations. A <strong>membership</strong> defines the relationship between a user and an organization, including their role.</p>

  <h3>How to invite members</h3>
  <p>You can invite anyone by email — they don't need an existing account.</p>
  <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
    <li>Go to <strong>Settings</strong> in the sidebar</li>
    <li>In the <strong>Invitations</strong> section, enter the person's email and select a role</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Send Invitation</strong></li>
    <li>The person receives an email with a link to accept the invitation</li>
    <li>If they already have an account, they are added immediately. If not, the link takes them to sign up and auto-joins the organization after registration.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Pending invitations can be cancelled before they are accepted.</p>

  <h3>Managing members</h3>
  <p>To change a member's role, use the role dropdown next to their name in the members table. To remove a member, click the <strong>Remove</strong> button.</p>

  <h2>Roles</h2>
  <p>Datanika uses four roles with hierarchical permissions. Roles are enforced on every action — attempting a restricted action shows "Permission denied."</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Permissions</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>Owner</code></td><td>Full access including org settings, billing, and member management.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Admin</code></td><td>Everything except org settings and billing. Can manage members, delete resources, import configs, and manage API keys.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Editor</code></td><td>Create and edit connections, uploads, pipelines, transformations, schedules. Trigger runs. Cannot delete or manage members.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Viewer</code></td><td>Read-only. Can view all resources, runs, logs, and the data catalog. Cannot create, edit, or delete anything.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Detailed Permission Matrix</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Owner</th><th>Admin</th><th>Editor</th><th>Viewer</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>View all resources</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Create/edit connections</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Create/edit uploads, pipelines, transformations</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Create/edit schedules</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Trigger runs</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Delete resources</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Manage members &amp; invitations</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Import configurations</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Manage API keys</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Organization settings</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Billing &amp; subscriptions</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Authentication</h2>
  <p>Users can sign in with:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Email & password</strong> — standard registration with an email address and a password</li>
    <li><strong>Google OAuth</strong> — sign in with your Google account</li>
    <li><strong>GitHub OAuth</strong> — sign in with your GitHub account</li>
    <li><strong>SSO (SAML / OIDC)</strong> — Enterprise plan only. Configured per organization.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>OAuth and SSO users are automatically created on first sign-in. No separate registration step is needed.</p>

  <h3>Email verification</h3>
  <p>Datanika does not require email verification. An account registered with an email address and password is usable immediately — no confirmation step gates sign-in, on either the cloud or self-hosted edition. Accounts created through Google, GitHub or SSO carry the address the provider already confirmed.</p>
  <p>If you want provider-verified identity for everyone in your organization, use SSO (Enterprise) or restrict your team to Google or GitHub sign-in.</p>

  <h3>Changing your password</h3>
  <p>Go to <strong>Settings → Your account</strong>. Enter your current password, then the new one twice. Passwords must be at least 8 characters and no more than 72 bytes — accented characters and emoji count as more than one byte each. There are no character-class rules; length is what matters.</p>
  <p>Changing your password does not sign you out of this browser, and it does not revoke your organization's API keys. Those are machine credentials with their own revocation screen — see <strong>Settings → API Keys</strong> if you think someone else may have had access to your account.</p>

  <h3>If you signed in with Google or GitHub</h3>
  <p>Accounts created through a social provider have no password, so <strong>Settings → Your account</strong> offers to <em>set</em> one instead of changing one — no current password required. Setting a password does not unlink the provider; you keep both ways in. This matters if you ever lose access to the Google or GitHub account you signed up with.</p>

  <h3>Resetting a forgotten password</h3>
  <p>Select <strong>Forgot your password?</strong> on the sign-in screen and enter your email address. If that address has an account, a link to set a new password arrives by email.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The link <strong>expires after 60 minutes</strong> and can be used <strong>only once</strong>.</li>
    <li>Requesting a new link <strong>invalidates any earlier one</strong>, so always use the most recent email.</li>
    <li>An expired, used, or superseded link all show the same message. Request a new one.</li>
    <li>The confirmation screen looks the same whether or not the address has an account. It is not telling you the address is wrong — check it for typos, and check your spam folder.</li>
    <li>After setting a new password you are returned to the sign-in screen and sign in with it. The link never signs you in by itself.</li>
    <li>Accounts that signed up with Google or GitHub can use this too — it is how you add a password without going through Settings.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Self-hosted: password reset needs SMTP</h3>
  <p>Reset by email requires a configured mail server. On an instance where <code>SMTP_HOST</code> is empty — which is the default — the <strong>Forgot your password?</strong> link is hidden, because the flow could only ever say "check your inbox" for an email that was never sent. Configure SMTP to enable it.</p>
  <p>Changing a password from <strong>Settings → Your account</strong> works with no mail server at all, so a self-hosted instance is never stuck: an administrator with access to the app can always change their own password.</p>

  <h3>Changing your email address</h3>
  <p>There is <strong>no self-service control for this yet</strong>. Your sign-in address is set at signup and cannot be edited in the product. We are building it; until then, email <a href="mailto:info@datanika.io">info@datanika.io</a> and we will change it for you.</p>
  <p>Saying this plainly rather than leaving the Settings page to imply it: if you go looking for the control, it is not hidden somewhere you have not found — it does not exist.</p>

  <h3>If you cannot receive mail at your account's address</h3>
  <p>A typo at signup, or a work address you have since lost, puts you in the one state the product cannot get you out of on its own: password reset sends a link to an address you do not control, and there is no way to change that address yourself. The route out depends on whether you can still sign in.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>You know your password and can sign in.</strong> You are fine — nothing about the wrong address stops you using Datanika. Email us to have the address corrected. Because you can sign in, we can confirm it is your account by asking you to make a small, specific change inside it and then observing that change.</li>
    <li><strong>You cannot sign in either.</strong> Email us from any address, name the organization, and expect to be asked for details only an account holder would know — organization name, the names of connections or pipelines you created, and billing details if you are on a paid plan. We would rather be slow here than hand an account to the wrong person.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>On <strong>self-hosted</strong> instances there is no "us" — an administrator with database access changes the address directly, and an administrator who can reach the app can always change their own password from Settings.</p>

  <h3>Deleting your account or your organization</h3>
  <p>There is <strong>no delete button in the product yet</strong>. Account and organization deletion is handled by us on request: email <a href="mailto:info@datanika.io">info@datanika.io</a> and we will respond within 30 days. This is the same route described in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>, and it is the one that is live today.</p>
  <p>What to expect when you ask:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Deleted:</strong> your name, email address, sign-in details, API keys and personal notification settings.</li>
    <li><strong>Kept:</strong> billing records, for 7 years, because tax law requires it.</li>
    <li><strong>Untouched:</strong> anything a pipeline already loaded into <em>your own</em> data warehouse. We never delete from your warehouse — that data is yours and we are only the processor for it.</li>
    <li><strong>Backups</strong> are kept for up to 30 days and then expire. We do not edit backups, so the 30 days is the real outer bound, not the deletion itself.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If you are the <strong>only owner of an organization that has other members</strong>, we will ask you to choose first: transfer ownership to someone else, or delete the organization along with your account. Deleting your account on its own would leave that organization with nobody able to administer it, so we will not do it silently.</p>
  <p>If your organization has no other members, it is deleted with your account — its connections, pipelines, uploads, schedules and run history go with it.</p>

  <h3>SSO (Enterprise)</h3>
  <p>Organizations on the Enterprise plan can configure SAML or OIDC single sign-on. This allows team members to authenticate via your company's identity provider (Okta, Azure AD, Google Workspace, etc.).</p>
  <p>SSO is configured in <strong>Settings</strong> by an organization owner or admin. Each organization can have one active SSO configuration.</p>

  <h3>Bot Protection</h3>
  <p>Login and signup forms are protected by reCAPTCHA v3 (invisible, score-based) when configured. This prevents brute-force attacks and automated abuse without adding friction for real users.</p>
</DocsLayout>
